  "content": {
    "id": "p37-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "Show that among thirteen points located inside a rectangle of length 6 and width 8, at least two points are no more than $2\\sqrt{2}$ units apart.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Divide the rectangle into twelve $2\\times2$ squares. Two of the thirteen points must lie in the same square, so their distance is at most its diagonal, $2\\sqrt{2}$ units.",
      "worked": "Divide the rectangle into three rows and four columns of $2\\times2$ squares, giving $3\\times4=12$ squares. Assign each point to one square; assign points on dividing lines consistently to just one adjacent square.\n\nBy the pigeonhole principle, thirteen points in twelve squares guarantee that one square contains at least two points. Their distance cannot exceed that square's diagonal:\n$$\\begin{align*}d&\\le\\sqrt{2^2+2^2}\\\\d&\\le2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nThus at least two points are no more than $2\\sqrt{2}$ units apart.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 40
  },
